{
  "requirement": "Each public SDK item has one defining module path. The SDK exposes no public\nre-exports. The chapter that introduces each supported type or free function owns its declaration.\nThe [public API inventory](sdk/public-api.md) links each item to that requirement,\nwhich describes its purpose\nand declares its public Rust interface, including fields, enum variants, trait\nmembers, and inherent methods. A change to that interface requires a change to\nthe owning requirement. Every supported struct with public fields is\n`#[non_exhaustive]`. Its constructor accepts the values needed to create it.\nAdditional fields have consuming builder methods that accept a concrete value.\nOptional fields start as `None`. Backend inference options start empty and can\nbe added one key at a time.\n\nThe SDK’s public modules follow the interfaces introduced in these chapters.\n\n- `domain`: application data and the `Domain`, `NodeContents`, and `SourceNodes` traits.\n- `graph`: criterion definitions, graph observations, and evidence; `graph::validator`\n  runs criteria.\n- `request`: `ValidationContext`, typed `Ask` builders, context values, and request phases.\n- `agent`: configuration for the supplied agents.\n- `sources`: project inspection, search queries, and their results.\n- `outline`: selectable source contents and language rules; `outline::languages`\n  supplies bundled languages, and `outline::selector` defines selector syntax.\n- `storage`: filesystem evidence storage.\n\nSource access, search engines, redaction, provider transports, agent-result paging,\nand conversation machinery are implementation details of those interfaces.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The reviewed tests adequately check `requirements.sdk.public_api`. I found no test-adequacy gap. The obligations map to tests as follows.\n\n**One defining path and no public re-exports.**\n- `tests/public_api.rs#fn[sdk_has_no_public_reexports]` parses every `.rs` file under `src` with tree-sitter. It fails on any `use_declaration` with a plain `pub` visibility modifier. It also asserts that each file parses without errors.\n- `fn[public_exports_match_the_appendix]` also covers this. It regenerates rustdoc for the current source tree and walks the module item tables. It fails on an unrecognized export category, on a duplicate `(path, kind)` export, and on any missing or unexpected path or kind compared with the inventory.\n\n**Inventory links each item to its owning requirement, and an interface change requires a requirement change.**\n- `fn[specified_exports]` parses the `public-api.md` inventory. It requires each non-module entry to link to a narrative chapter (not `public-api.md`). It requires the anchor to match the chapter's `requirements.sdk.*` namespace. It rejects duplicate members and malformed nesting.\n- `fn[documented_exports]` calls `fn[check_declaration]` for every rustdoc item. `check_declaration` reads the owning `{req}` block and requires a prose contract and a `rust,ignore` declaration. It then compares four things against rustdoc:\n  - trait implementations;\n  - the set of indexed member names (fields, methods, associated types and consts);\n  - the declaration text, which covers fields, variants and trait members;\n  - the normalized inherent method signatures.\n- Any added, removed or changed signature produces a \"differs\" failure.\n- `fn[member_order_is_free_but_field_and_method_types_are_committed]` checks that this comparison tolerates method reordering. It also checks that it rejects a changed field type, a changed return type, and a change from `&mut self` to `&self`.\n\n**Public-field structs are `#[non_exhaustive]`.** `check_declaration` asserts that any struct page with `structfield.` entries has `#[non_exhaustive]` in its rustdoc declaration. This runs over every exported struct.\n\n**Constructors take needed values, and builders consume `self` and take concrete values.** These obligations are enforced through the signature comparison against the requirement declarations. The comparison catches a builder that takes `&mut self` or a generic parameter in place of the declared concrete type. Behavior is exercised in `agent/config.rs#mod[tests]/fn[backend_builders_set_only_the_requested_fields]`. That test covers the Bedrock, OpenAI and Ollama constructors and every builder (`aws_profile`, `region`, `credentials`, `session_token`, `model`, `endpoint`, `api_key_env`, `api_key`, `option`). It runs them in consuming chains and checks the resulting JSON.\n\n**Optional fields start as `None`.**\n- The config test checks that a freshly built `BedrockConfig`, `OpenAiConfig`, `OllamaConfig` and `BedrockCredentials` serialize with no optional keys. Those fields are marked `skip_serializing_if = \"Option::is_none\"`, so this shows they start as `None`.\n- `tests/source_defaults.rs` asserts `query.limit == None` for `TextQuery` and `StructuralQuery`. It asserts `context_lines == 0` and `file.language == None` for `SourceFile::new`.\n- `Outline::new`'s `diagnostic: None` is asserted elsewhere. `Record::new` takes its optional `version` as an explicit constructor argument, so it has no default to test.\n\n**Backend inference options start empty and are added one key at a time.** The config test checks that `options` is absent initially. It then adds `a` and `b` one key at a time, and a repeated `a` replaces the earlier value (`{\"a\": 2, \"b\": true}`). It does the same for the OpenAI and Ollama configs.\n\n**Public module layout.** The inventory-versus-rustdoc comparison checks every module and item the SDK exposes. This includes `graph::validator`, `outline::languages` and `outline::selector`. Any module that is missing, extra or misplaced fails `public_exports_match_the_appendix`.\n\nThe remaining prose says source access, search engines and similar machinery are implementation details. That is satisfied when nothing outside the inventory is exported, which the exports test enforces.\n\nThe `source_defaults.rs` tests marked `requirements.sdk.public_api` exercise the builders and defaults of the `sources` types. They assert concrete values.",
  "references": "1f6a63bba43a7144abcfe7cd798bf312674830f0f1259db427607f5c6160a0cb",
